# Reports directory — security audit outputs

Generated artefacts live here. They are intentionally **not** committed to
version control (see `.gitignore`); only this `.gitkeep` file is tracked.

Each scanner writes a dated file:

| File pattern | Producer |
|--------------|----------|
| `pip-audit-YYYY-MM-DD.json` | `pip-audit.sh` |
| `safety-YYYY-MM-DD.json` | `safety.sh` |
| `bandit-YYYY-MM-DD.json` | `bandit.sh` |
| `secrets-YYYY-MM-DD.txt` | `secrets-scan.sh` |
| `trivy-YYYY-MM-DD.json` | `trivy.sh` |
| `headers-YYYY-MM-DD.md` | `headers-check.sh` |
| `cors-YYYY-MM-DD.md` | `cors-check.sh` |
| `authn-YYYY-MM-DD.md` | `authn-check.sh` |
| `rate-limit-YYYY-MM-DD.md` | `rate-limit-check.sh` |
| `zap-baseline-YYYY-MM-DD.html` | `owasp-zap.sh` |
| `zap-baseline-YYYY-MM-DD.json` | `owasp-zap.sh` |
| `<tool>-YYYY-MM-DD.log` | `run-all.sh` (per-tool stdout) |
| `security-audit-YYYY-MM-DD.md` | `run-all.sh` (aggregated) |

These reports may contain vulnerabilities, secret-material candidates, or
internal hostnames. Treat them as **confidential** and never publish them
in public artifacts or pastebins.